devel/ -- published, but never going to qemu-devel
==================================================

Patches needed by anyone who builds or modifies sun4v firmware against QEMU, and
by nobody else. They are published (mirrored to github.com/unix0cc/qemu-experimental
under devel/) because they are useful to others doing the same work, but they are
not proposed upstream and are not written to upstream's standards for inclusion.

Contrast with the sibling directories:

  upstream/  posted or intended for qemu-devel; numbered as a series; must stay
             byte-identical to the published copy
  devel/     this directory; same byte-identity rule
  test/      experiment-local, never published, deleted when the experiment ends

The byte-identity rule matters: the published copy is the canonical location of a
patch, and a local edit that is not mirrored makes the two silently disagree.
Regenerate SHA256SUMS after editing anything here.

The `0003` prefix (2026-08-13) is a stack-application-order number shared
across upstream/, devel/ and test/ -- see test/README-vpci-stack -- not a
qemu-devel series number. Only 0002 is actually posted; this directory is
never proposed at all, regardless of what its filename says.

Contents
--------
0003-niagara-hvuart-console.patch
    Models the hypervisor's private console UART at 0xfff0c2c000, an address the
    machine's own hv MD has always declared (`hvuart` in the root node) and which
    QEMU did not decode. Required to run a self-built hypervisor at all: such a
    build hangs on its first PRINT(). Harmless to existing setups -- the stock
    blob is a non-DEBUG build and writes nothing to the port.

0023-niagara-strand-halt-erratum-39.patch
    Implements the strand halt on the wr-form write to %asr26 (STRAND_STS_REG):
    do_wrssr gets a real TCG helper that masks the write to the two writable
    fields and, when .active clears, actually halts the strand (cs->halted = 1)
    instead of being a plain register store with no side effect. Also assigns
    %hver at reset (never assigned anywhere in stock QEMU), because the Niagara
    erratum #39 guard in plat_halt_strand() checks %hver before issuing the write
    at all and always saw 0 -- the oldest possible part -- and skipped the halt
    outright. Needs 0010 (test/, asr26-strand-status-write) applied first: that
    one adds the missing wr-form decode entry, this one is what makes the write
    it decodes actually do something. Without both, an idle-strand boot spins
    cpu_yield at 100% host CPU forever instead of halting.

0024-niagara-wake-halted-strand-on-pending-irq.patch
    Fixes sparc_cpu_has_work() so a strand halted by 0023 can wake again. The
    upstream function (untouched since 2013, and never reached before 0023
    because nothing else halts a SPARC64 strand) only asks whether an
    interrupt is *deliverable right now* -- CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD raised and
    interrupts enabled. A halted strand wakes on a *pending* interrupt
    instead: PSTATE.IE gates trap delivery once the strand resumes, not
    whether the halt may end. The halt is routinely entered with IE = 0 (an
    ordinary check-then-sleep idle loop), so a %stick tick that fires and is
    correctly deferred by cpu_check_irqs() in the instant before the halt
    leaves SOFTINT.STIMER set with nothing to deliver it -- has_work()
    returns false, the strand is never re-evaluated, the one-shot %stick_cmpr
    is never re-armed, and it parks forever. This is "Signature B" in the
    notes repo ([[signature-b-clean-idle-stall]]): caught live and
    simultaneously on two guests, identical state, permanently frozen. Fix:
    when cs->halted, report work if any raw source is pending (iqueue
    non-empty, pil_in, softint, or CPU_INTERRUPT_HARD). Needs 0004
    (test/, mondo-queues-asi-queue) for env->iqueue and the IQUEUE_* offsets,
    and 0023. A >9 h idle that froze deterministically before now stays
    healthy on both the cmd646/IDE and mptsas/PCIe stacks.

Patches this stack needs only because of our own hv
-----------------------------------------------------
Four patches across this stack exist because our own T2/Ontario hypervisor
build is the first thing to exercise a given upstream QEMU code path, not
because of a generic Solaris-on-sun4v or QEMU bug. Traceable here since they
otherwise look unrelated and are scattered across devel/ and test/:

  0003 (this directory)      hv's own PRINT() console -- hangs on the first
                              call without it. Needed by any self-built hv,
                              not by the stock blob.
  0010 (test/)                the wr-form %asr26 write our hv's
                              plat_halt_strand() (hcall_cpu.s) uses to idle a
                              strand -- traps illegal_instruction without it.
  0023 (this directory)      the erratum #39 guard in that same
                              plat_halt_strand(), and the halt behaviour behind
                              the write 0010 only decodes.
  0024 (this directory)      the wake half: sparc_cpu_has_work() must check
                              raw pending sources, not deliverability, or a
                              strand halted by 0023 never resumes.

0010/0023/0024 are one mechanism -- decode the halt write, make it halt,
make the halt wake -- split across three patches by how they were found:
0010 landed first, before the halt itself was known to be missing; 0024 last,
after Signature B was root-caused. See [[cpu-yield-strand-halt-not-implemented]],
[[qemu-hver-never-assigned]] and [[signature-b-clean-idle-stall]] in the notes
repo. Apply in order 0010 -> 0023 -> 0024; each is inert or worse without the
one before it.

The common thread: none of these four are things a stock or third-party
sun4v guest/hv combination would ever hit. They surface specifically because
this project builds its own hypervisor from source (OpenSPARC T2/Ontario) and
runs it under QEMU rather than only running prebuilt Sun/Oracle firmware --
anyone doing the same will need this exact set, and 0003/0010/0023/0024 is the
reason devel/ exists as a category separate from upstream/ at all.
